findings.body:
The loop bound changed from `j < numkeys` to `j <= numkeys`, so it runs one extra time on every keyed command in cluster mode:

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1109-1126"}

On that last iteration (`j == numkeys`):
- `keys_result->keys[numkeys]` is one entry past the valid key references. It's either stale data in `keysbuf` or past the end of a heap allocation, so `.pos` is garbage.
- `argv[garbage]` can be out of bounds, NULL, or simply a different argument, such as `argv[0]` (the command name) if the stale `pos` is 0.
- `slot_buffer[numkeys]` writes 4 bytes past the end of a `malloc(sizeof(int) * numkeys)` block. That's a heap buffer overflow.

::callout{type="warning"}
For a plain `GET foo` in cluster mode, the extra iteration hashes whatever argv entry the garbage `pos` points to. If that entry hashes to a different slot, the command is reported as `CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT` and rejected with a CROSSSLOT error. If `pos` is wild, the server segfaults. This runs on the hot `preprocessCommand()` path, and now on the ASM path too, through `getSlotFromCommand()`.

The bound has to go back to `<`.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1111-1111"}
    for (int j = 0; j < keys_result->numkeys; j++) {

`slot_buffer` is written but never read. All it adds is a heap allocation and a free on every keyed command during preprocessing:

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1126"}

It also has three other problems:
- It calls raw `malloc`/`free` instead of `zmalloc`/`zfree`, so the memory isn't counted in `used_memory`. Redis code consistently uses the `z*` allocators.
- The `malloc` result isn't checked for NULL.
- Every early-return path has to remember to `free` the buffer, which is exactly how leaks creep in later.

Delete it entirely; the old code tracked only `first_slot`.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1127"}
    int first_slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT;
    for (int j = 0; j < keys_result->numkeys; j++) {
        robj *this_key = argv[keys_result->keys[j].pos];
        int this_slot = (int)keyHashSlot((char*)this_key->ptr, sdslen(this_key->ptr));

        if (first_slot == INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT)
            first_slot = this_slot;
        else if (first_slot != this_slot)
            return CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT;
    }
    return first_slot;

Before this PR, `extractKeysAndSlot()` always set `*slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT` up front. Now `*slot` is written only when `num_keys > 0`:

::diff{file="src/db.c" lines="3201-3204"}

The one current caller (`preprocessCommand`, server.c:4110) happens to set `pcmd->slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT` beforehand, so nothing breaks today. But the function is exported in server.h, and a future caller passing an uninitialized `int slot;` would get garbage for keyless commands. Restoring the unconditional initialization makes the contract self-contained.

::suggestion{file="src/db.c" lines="3201-3204"}
    *slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT;
    if (num_keys > 0)
        *slot = extractSlotFromKeysResult(argv, result);
    return num_keys;

After `if (num_keys < 0) return;`, the condition `num_keys >= 0` can't be false, so the `else if` is noise and suggests a distinction that doesn't exist:

::diff{file="src/server.c" lines="4138-4148"}

